# Documentation Rules

## Content Guidelines
- Use Markdown for all documentation
- Structure documentation as a knowledge graph
- Provide clear entry points for each domain
- Link related documents explicitly
- Keep documentation concise and focused
- Use consistent terminology across all documentation

## Architecture Documentation
- Document overall system architecture
- Clearly identify components and their responsibilities
- Document integration points between components
- Include diagrams for complex relationships
- Update architecture documentation when changes occur

## Decision Records
- Document important decisions with context
- Include alternatives considered and trade-offs
- Link decisions to requirements or constraints
- Update or archive decisions when they change
- Use consistent format for all decision records

## User Guides
- Separate end-user documentation from developer documentation
- Include examples for common operations
- Provide troubleshooting information
- Update guides when functionality changes
- Consider both technical and non-technical audiences

## Code Documentation
- Write self-documenting code with clear naming
- Document public APIs and interfaces thoroughly
- Document any non-obvious implementation details
- Include examples for complex functionality
- Mark technical debt clearly with TODO comments
